NAME
       journalctl - read the systemd journal (the system log)

SYNOPSIS
       journalctl [OPTION...] [FIELD=VALUE...]

DESCRIPTION
       systemd-journald collects messages from the kernel, from services
       (whatever they print and send to syslog) and from the boot itself,
       with the time, host, program, process ID, unit and priority of each.
       journalctl shows them, oldest first, in a pager (less): use the arrow
       keys and / to search, q to quit. Normal users see their own messages;
       members of the wheel group (and adm, systemd-journal) and root see
       everything.

       rsyslog also writes most messages as text to /var/log/messages,
       /var/log/secure (logins, sudo), /var/log/cron and others.

   Keeping the journal
       By default RHEL keeps the journal in /run/log/journal, which is in
       memory and is lost at every reboot (Storage=auto with no
       /var/log/journal directory). To keep it on disk across reboots,
       either create the directory or set it in the configuration:

           mkdir -p /var/log/journal          (with Storage=auto)
           or: Storage=persistent in /etc/systemd/journald.conf
           then: systemctl restart systemd-journald

       After that, journalctl --list-boots shows earlier boots and -b -1
       shows the previous one.

   Priorities
       0 emerg, 1 alert, 2 crit, 3 err, 4 warning, 5 notice, 6 info,
       7 debug. -p err shows err and everything more serious.

OPTIONS
       -u UNIT, --unit=UNIT
              Messages of one service, such as -u sshd or -u httpd.service.

       -b [N], --boot[=N]
              Messages of this boot only; -b -1 the previous boot (needs a
              persistent journal).

       --list-boots
              List the boots in the journal.

       -p PRIORITY, --priority=PRIORITY
              Only this priority and more serious; a range like -p
              warning..err works too.

       -S TIME, --since=TIME; -U TIME, --until=TIME
              Only messages from (or up to) TIME: "2026-03-02 09:00",
              "09:30", today, yesterday, "1 hour ago", "-10min".

       -n [N], --lines[=N]
              Only the last N lines (10 by default).

       -f, --follow
              Keep showing new messages as they arrive (Ctrl+C stops).

       -r, --reverse
              Newest first.

       -e, --pager-end
              Jump to the end in the pager.

       -x, --catalog
              Add explanations to messages where the catalog has them.

       -k, --dmesg
              Only kernel messages (of this boot).

       -t ID, --identifier=ID
              Only messages with this syslog identifier, such as -t sudo or
              the tag of logger -t.

       -g PATTERN, --grep=PATTERN
              Only messages whose text matches the regular expression.

       -o FORMAT, --output=FORMAT
              short (the default), short-iso, short-precise, verbose (every
              field), json, json-pretty, cat (only the text).

       --no-pager
              Print straight to the terminal.

       -q, --quiet
              No informational lines such as "-- No entries --".

       --disk-usage
              How much space the journal files use.

       --vacuum-size=SIZE, --vacuum-time=TIME
              Delete old journal files until they use less than SIZE, or
              those older than TIME.

       --flush
              Move the journal from /run to /var/log/journal now.

       --rotate
              Start new journal files.

       --utc  Show times in UTC.

   Matching fields
       FIELD=VALUE arguments show only entries with that field, for example
       _PID=1234, _UID=1000, _COMM=sshd, _SYSTEMD_UNIT=sshd.service,
       PRIORITY=3, SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER=sudo. -o verbose shows all fields of an
       entry.

EXAMPLES
       Why did the web server not start?

           systemctl status httpd
           journalctl -u httpd -n 20

       Errors since this boot:

           journalctl -b -p err

       What happened in the last hour, newest first:

           journalctl --since "1 hour ago" -r

       Follow sshd while someone logs in from server2:

           journalctl -u sshd -f

       Messages a script sent with logger -t backup:

           journalctl -t backup

       Save today's warnings to a file:

           journalctl --since today -p warning --no-pager > /root/warnings.txt

FILES
       /etc/systemd/journald.conf
              Settings (Storage=, SystemMaxUse=...).

       /run/log/journal, /var/log/journal
              Where the journal is kept (in memory, or on disk).

SEE ALSO
       systemd-journald(8), journald.conf(5), logger(1), systemctl(1),
       rsyslogd(8)
